AD  - University of Malaya, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
AB  - Biological databases are digitised collections of information on life sciences which includes omics (genomics, proteomics, transcriptomics and metabolomics) and other datasets such as microarray gene expressions, phylogenetics, taxonomy, animal models, disease and clinical. These datasets are captured and collated from many different sources including scientific experiments using high throughput technologies, published literatures and computational technologies. Biological data are expected to grow in the coming years as high end technologies are being used to generate high volumes of data, thus data storage strategies and solutions need to cope with the trend in order to maximise data utilisation in mining, visualizing and knowledge extraction. This article reflects upon techniques of data modeling, architecture with a highlight on the uses of databases. Examples of biological databases in different domains of biology is presented, focusing on two case studies. Finally the future of biological databases is discussed to give an overview of the modeling techniques to accommodate the biological data escalation in coming years. © 2019 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
