AD  - LION Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, United Kingdom
AB  - The sequence retrieval system (SRS) approach to data integration has evolved over many years to address the needs of researchers in life sciences-that is, to query, retrieve, and analyze complex, ever increasing, and changing biological data. SRSs follow a federation approach to data integration, leaving the underlying data sources in their original formats. For example, GenBank is used in flat file format, and the genome ontology is used in either XML format or as relational tables stored in MySQL. Databanks generated and provided by the major technologies available are integrated through meta-data, which is provided for the majority of the common public data sources. SRS customers use this functionality to integrate their own in-house data (such as gene expression databases) with third-party data (such as Incyte LifeSeq Foundation data) and public data (such as EMBL and Swiss-Prot). SRSs aim at remaining independent of the technology used for data storage. Extensible markup language (XML), flat files, and relational databases bring with them a range of benefits and problems that often create a particular mind-set for the people who use and maintain them. © 2003 by Elsevier Science (USA). All rights reserved.
